This section is a SQL-injection hardening fix to `order_by()`, not an authorization change. The change tightens the deprecated raw-alias branch so that only strings matching `ORDER_PATTERN` (`[-+]?[.\w]+$`) are treated as raw column aliases; anything else (e.g. `queries.name);`) now falls through to `names_to_path()`, which raises `FieldError`. This reduces, not increases, the attack surface. There is no user-controlled object identifier being fetched, no protected operation lacking an auth/role check, and no incorrect authorization comparison introduced. The retrieved policy excerpts concern test ordering, `order_by` semantics, and the auth framework — none establish an authorization rule that this change violates. No candidate authorization flaw is present.

